<td id="S4.T3.2.4.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">7.39</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">ExQPE M scaled by <math id="S4.T3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S4.T3.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">2.74</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">2.69</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T3.2.5.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.65</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T3.2.5.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.76</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.5.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T3.2.5.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4.98</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span> All results are for ExPE Medium-sized models. The legend (ev=1) is the evaluation on texts of length equal to the training length. Here, for all models, the training length is 512 tokens. (ev=2) is the evaluation on texts with length double the training length, etc.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We also evaluated the performance of the models on the HellaSwag <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zellers et al. (2019)</a></cite>, MMLU(Measuring Massive Multitask Language Understanding) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al. (2021)</a></cite>, ARC and ARC easy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moskvichev et al. (2023)</a></cite> benchmarks to test how they perform on standard LLM benchmarks and we see that their results (found in Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.2 Large-scale Experiments ‣ 4 Experiments and Results ‣ ExPe: Exact Positional Encodings for Generative Transformer Models with Extrapolating Capabilities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) are comparable. In terms of training inference speed and memory requirements, ExPE is practically equivalent to Sinusoidal, as shown by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Press et al. (2022)</a></cite>; they are significantly faster than RoPE. In general, even if ExPE doesn’t demonstrate a remarkable ability to extrapolate to sequences longer than those seen during training, it still shows significant improvement compared to RoPe and Sinusoidal. Also manages to maintain performance compared to RoPe while requiring significantly less compute when compared to sequence lengths seen during training.</p>
</div>
